respuesta_texto:
  Sure! Here is an example of a C++ program that uses recursion to find the nth Fibonacci number:
```
#include <iostream>

int fibonacci(int n) {
  if (n == 0) {
    return 0;
  } else if (n == 1) {
    return 1;
  } else {
    return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);
  }
int main() {
  std::cout << "Fibonacci number of n = " << 5 << std::endl;
  std::cout << fibonacci(5) << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```
This program defines a function `fibonacci` that takes an integer `n` as input and returns the `n`th Fibonacci number. The function uses recursion to calculate the Fibonacci number, by calling itself with the argument `n-1` and `n-2` until it reaches the base case where `n` is 0 or 1, in which case the function returns the appropriate value.
In the `main` function, we call the `fibonacci` function with the argument `5` and print the result to the console.
Note that this program will only work for positive integers `n`. If you want to handle negative integers or other types of inputs, you will need to modify the program accordingly.

