  Sure, here is a simple website in HTML that displays a random joke when a user clicks a button:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Random Joke Website</title>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Random Joke Website</h1>
  <button id="button">Click me!</button>
  <script>
    // Define an array of jokes
    const jokes = [
      "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything.",
      "I told my wife she was drawing her eyebrows too high. She looked surprised.",
      "Why don't lobsters share? Because they're shellfish.",
      "What do you call a fake noodle? An impasta."
    ];

    // Function to generate a random joke
    function getRandomJoke() {
      // Get a random number between 0 and the length of the array - 1
      const randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
      // Return the joke at the selected position
      return jokes[randomNumber];
    }

    // Add an event listener to the button
    document.getElementById("button").addEventListener("click", getRandomJoke);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```
This code creates a simple web page with a button that, when clicked, will display a random joke from an array of four jokes. The joke is generated using the `getRandomJoke()` function, which takes a random number between 0 and the length of the array - 1, and returns the joke at that position.
You can save this code in a file with an `.html` extension and open it in a web browser to see the random joke display when you click the button.
Note: This is just a basic example, you can add more jokes to the array, or use a different method to generate the random joke.

